Working with Data in Splunk

When looking at data from different sources within Splunk, you can identify the source based on some default fields:

1
The host is the name of the physical or virtual device where an event originates from. For example: a Linux or Windows endpoint, or a firewall, proxy, etc.

2
The source is the name of the file, directory, data stream, or other input from which a particular event originates. For example, for data monitored from files and directories, the source consists of the full pathname of the file or directory.

3
Sources are classified into sourcetypes, which can be well-known formats or formats defined by a Splunk Admin.



The "sourcetype" is a default field that identifies the data structure of an event. It determines how the Splunk platform formats the data during the indexing process and how it will be displayed back to you during searches. 


If you are going to start exploring a new environment, a great place to start is to take a look at the different types of hosts and sources that we are receiving data from. Let's review some commands that can help you with that. 


Browsing indexes
Let's begin with getting oriented to the data we have. Our data is contained within indexes. 

In Splunk, an index is a repository for data. When the Splunk platform indexes raw data, it transforms the data into searchable events. The indexes themselves reside in flat files on the indexer.
For example, we can search for all indexes (*), count them, and display them in a table with the index name as the visible field. This way we can see all the indexes with searchable events and narrow our search to a specific index when needed. 

index=* | stats count by index | fields index


Browsing hosts
The metadata command returns a list of sources, sourcetypes, or hosts from a specified index or distributed search peer. 

In the example below, the index is specified as a wildcard (*), so that we can view a full list of hosts, regardless of where the indexed data resides.
With the following commands, we specify the type (hosts), and a wildcard to search all available indexes. 

| metadata type=hosts  index=*


Browsing sourcetypes
Let's run the metadata command while filtering for sourcetypes:
| metadata type=sourcetypes  index=*


Browsing sources
And sources, we can't forget about those! Check out the sources available for Frothly Brewery.
| metadata type=sources  index=*


Splunk supports built-in or pre-trained source types that it uses to parse incoming data into events. You can see a few of these in Frothly's on-premise environment. 
You can read about them in Splunk Documentation(opens in a new tab).

Some Splunk add-ons have preconfigured inputs that set the appropriate source type for the third-party technology. The add-on separates the data into specific source types if there is more than one source type included with it.


While analysts quickly become familiar with various types of logs, no one can be an expert on ALL of them. 
This is why normalization of data is important, it means that all the data received should follow a common classification scheme
so that Analysts like us can work with all types of data without having to know the exact structure and syntax of every single source we investigate.

The Splunk Common Information Model helps address this problem by providing a standard to map fields from various data sources. 


Data Models

Data models are a group of standard field names and values that can make working with multiple data sources so much easier. 
If you think about the "junk drawer" example from before, a data model is the equivalent of labeling all the boxes in your drawer and strictly following that label for the contents of all the boxes in the drawer. 
This means the next time you are looking for scissors, a stapler, or tape, you know exactly which box in the drawer to search. 

the Common Information Model (or CIM) is basically a collection of data models that Splunk defined for specific types of data that are most relevant to security, and generally found in an enterprise environment.




Data Models for Analysis

Authentication, Data Access, Databases, Data Loss Prevention, Email, Endpoint, Intrusion Detection, Malware, Network Traffic, Vulnerabilities, and Web are data models commonly used in security investigations, 
but ultimately the information you have and what you are investigating will dictate the data models that can add value to your efforts.



Authentication Data Model
This data model helps with the correlation of authentication-related data from various sources, grouping data such as successful versus failed authentications, use of default credentials, or logons from privileged users, to name a few.

Email Data model
This data model helps with analyzing and investigating email-related logs for various sources. It can help identify information like the source or destination of emails, file hashes for email attachments, subject lines, or other information about the email message or contents.

Endpoint Data Model
This data model provides insights about activities on the endpoint, such as file access, process execution, network connections, and other system changes.

Malware Data model
related to malware events including indicators like malware signatures, file hashes, and actions taken through the data reporting system such as an anti-virus blocking a connection or deleting a file.

Dataloss Prevention Model
This data model describes events gathered from DLP tools used to identify, monitor and protect data. This may include information such as data transfers, unauthorized file access, or the transfer of sensitive data across the network.

Network Traffic Model
This data model describes flows of data across network infrastructure components. Analysts may use it to interpret logs related to network traffic and identify information like IP addresses, ports, TCP flags, and traffic volumes to name a few.



Splunk Search Processing Language (SPL)
Splunk Search Processing Language (or SPL) encompasses search commands and the functions, arguments, and clauses used to tell Splunk what to do with the events retrieved from the storage indexes. 

Using SPL is fundamental to investigating events in Splunk as it can be used to search, filter, modify, manipulate, insert, and delete information, all at once. 
The better we get at using these SPL searches, the faster and more effective we can become as Analysts.

Below are some SPL commands that can help you with narrowing down your search. Click on each item to read how they may be used in an investigation.

The "transaction" command is handy when you need to group related events together, based on a common field, or even a set of fields. 

Let's think about the situation with the user BruceGist and potential data exfiltration. You want to analyze the sessions for this user interacting with a cloud storage service. 

You can see that we are filtering the search for the user and domain (dh) from our scenario with Bruce. 
The transaction command indicates that the fields of interest are the user, and the amount of data, in bytes, that was sent (bytes_out) based on the filtered events. 
The results of the transaction command are also sorted by size (bytes_out) and displayed in a table as shown in the image.
More information about the transaction command is available in Documentation(opens in a new tab).

The "top" and "rare" SPL commands in Splunk identify the most, or least frequent values. For example, you could use the top or rare commands to identify the most and the least common domains that are being queried within an environment. 

The "first" and "last" SPL commands are useful for analysts investigating an activity's first or last incidence. For example, the "first" command could pinpoint the first event in the original list of values returned. These commands provide chronological and timestamp order distinction of events.

You can use the rex command to either extract fields using regular expression named groups or replace or substitute characters in a field using sed expressions. Think about how useful this may be to an analyst!

 | rex field=_raw "From: <(?<from>.*)> To: <(?<to>.*)>"



