The Lookup command
The lookup command allows you to add data to Splunk, like a CSV file of known indicators, and then use that data to enhance your Splunk searches. Lookups can be imported CSV files, but they can also be automatic, scripted, pull data from databases, or leverage Splunk’s KV store. 

1. Create a CSV
2. Create a lookup table file from a csv
3. Create a lookup definition
4. Use your file in search


The required syntax is in bold.

lookup[local=<bool>][update=<bool>]<lookup-table-name>( <lookup-field> [AS <event-field>] )...[ OUTPUT | OUTPUTNEW (<lookup-destfield> [AS <event-destfield>] )... ] 
You can learn more about syntax for this, and other commands, in the Splunk Documentation(opens in a new tab).

Malicious activity over DNS may be indicative of things like an increase in the volume of requests by the client, 
a change in the type of resource records you see, variance in the length of the request, indicating encoding or obfuscation, variability in the frequency of requests, randomness in domain names, or substitution of domains to very slightly altered domains, as in typosquatting.

Eval is huge! The eval command can help you analyze and calculate results for mathematical, string, and boolean expressions.

the results of your calculation using eval are then written to a search results field. You can specify that field.  Results that are numbers and strings are going to be automatically assigned; if you have a boolean, you have to make sure that you convert it to a string using the function tostring(). 

In this example SPL, eval is used with the if() function to set the value in an "error" field to 'OK' if the status value is 200. Otherwise, the "error" field is set to 'Problem. '

index=main sourcetype=access_combined
| eval error = if(status == 200, "OK", "Problem")

Syntax for the eval command:

eval <field>=<expression>["," <field>=<expression>]... 
You can learn more about syntax for this, and other commands, in the Splunk Documentation(opens in a new tab).


Searching Data models

A common term you'll hear when working with Data Models is datasets. Datasets are collections of data with specific constraints and fields, making up each Data Model. 
While it may take practice and patience, it's important to become familiar with the structure of some of these datasets because you'll need to identify them if you want to use a Data Model in a search, or if you want to understand what an existing search is being used for. 

Accelerating a Data Model tells Splunk to keep a separate set of index files with all the accelerated datasets in it. By saving key values in a summary index, searches speed up because Splunk can then use the values from these index files, rather than having to retrieve the raw events for the search.

Data Model searches can become quite complex, so these are used more often by our Engineers or Architects when they build or modify dashboards, reports, or correlation searches for example.

| datamodel [<data model name>] [<data model search mode>] [summariesonly=<bool>]


The name of the data model to search. When only the data model is specified, the search returns the JSON for the single data model. This is helpful when you don't have easy access to the documentation to review the available datasets or fields. 
For example see the result of the following SPL below:
| datamodel authentication


You can use data model searches to return either results or a search string. If you want to do this, you must provide a <data model search mode>. 
You can use the "search" mode to return search results exactly how they are defined. 
Alternatively, you can use the "flat" mode to strip hierarchical information from the field names.


