Using Statistical Queries
Another command available for you to use on accelerated data models is tstats. The tstats command is used to perform statistical queries on indexed fields in a time-series index (tsidx) file. 

Together, the raw data file and its related tsidx file make up the contents of an index.
Because tstats searches on index-time fields instead of raw events, it ends up being faster than the stats command.

The tstats command calculates statistics based on fields in your events. The eval command creates new fields in your events by using existing fields and an arbitrary expression.
The stats command calculates aggregate statistics, such as average, count, and sum, over the incoming search results set. But how is it different from tstats?
Well, a key difference in what the command searches through. The tstats command only looks at the indexed metadata, whereas stats is working off the data (in this case the raw events) before that command.

The SPL search below uses tsats to show information about any processes started on endpoints. It is also organizing it based on the time the event appeared. 

This search uses summary information from the Endpoint Data Model in Splunk. You can see how the "Processes" dataset is being identified here to compose this search. 

| tstats summariesonly=true count from datamodel=Endpoint.Processes where Processes.user="*" Processes.process=* Processes.parent_process=* Processes.user="*" groupby _time span=1s Processes.process Processes.parent_process Processes.user |`drop_dm_object_name("Processes")`
| table _time process parent_process user count
| sort + _time

Better Searching Do this!

Restricting time ranges whenever possible.

•
Using appropriate index and sourcetype filters.

•
Using filters and sub-searches to narrow results and drill down to more granular details.


